findings.body:
`robj *argv[5]` is declared with 5 slots, but the code writes `argv[5] = c->argv[2]` and then passes `argc = 6` to `alsoPropagate()`. That is an out-of-bounds write on the stack, plus an out-of-bounds read inside `alsoPropagate()`. It runs on every HINCRBYFLOAT against a field with a TTL, which is the exact case this PR is meant to fix.

This is undefined behavior. It can corrupt adjacent stack data, trip stack protection, or crash the server. It can also appear to work in tests because the value happens to land in readable memory, so the passing replication test does not prove the code is correct.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2305-2314"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The array needs six slots: HPEXPIREAT, key, ts, FIELDS, count, field.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2306-2306"}
        robj *argv[6];

The check in `hashTypeGetValue()` changed from `expiredAt >= now` to `*expiredAt > now`. This is not needed for the fix and is not mentioned in the PR description. A field whose expiry equals the current command time is now treated as expired by every reader that goes through this helper (HGET, HEXISTS, HSTRLEN, HINCRBYFLOAT, and others), not only by HINCRBYFLOAT.

The new form matches the `val > info->now` check used in the listpack active-expiry code, so it may be intentional. If it is, the PR should say so and ideally cover the boundary in a test. If it is not, revert it to keep this change focused.

